  <data name="Info_HardwareAccelerationDesc" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Turning on hardware acceleration improves your battery life, performance, and responsiveness. Hardware acceleration offloads certain tasks from the CPU to the GPU or any other specialized hardware that can do it more efficiently, resulting in faster processing times and longer-lasting batteries.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Info_ImageFitType" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Image fit type</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Info_ImageFitTypeDesc" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Allow to embed multiple sizes in a file and set how image should be resized to fit image container:
• Contain: image is scaled to maintain its aspect ratio while fitting within the image's content box. The entire object is made to fill the box, while preserving its aspect ratio.
• Cover: image is resized to maintain its aspect ratio while filling the image's entire content box. If the object's aspect ratio does not match the aspect ratio of its box, then the object will be clipped to fit.
• Fill: image is resized to fill the image's content box. The entire image will completely fill the box. If the image's aspect ratio does not match the aspect ratio of its box, then the image will be stretched to fit.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Info_Interlaced" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Interlaced</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Info_InterlacedDesc" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Choose the way it compresses and displays image:
• Non-interlaced: image will load and display line by line from top to bottom.
• Interlaced: image is loaded from odd lines and displayed on odd lines, then continue to load and display on even lines.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Info_InterlacedProgressive" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Interlaced PNG and Progressive JPEG</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Info_InterlacedProgressiveDesc" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>INTERLACED (PNG)
• Non-interlaced PNG: image will load and display line by line from top to bottom.
• Interlaced PNG: image is loaded from odd lines and displayed on odd lines, then continue to load and display on even lines.
PROGRESSIVE (JPEG)
• Baseline JPEG: from top to bottom.
• Progressive JPEG: from blurry to sharp. This is ideal for large images that will be displayed while downloading over a slow connection, allowing a reasonable preview after receiving only a portion of the data.</value>
  </data>
